Contents
In the News
1. Humana=E2=80=99s Stars recovery creates a two-speed 2028 quality market
2. McKesson gains a path from specialty distribution into home-infusion owner=
ship
3. DOJ turns an unresolved diagnosis-deletion queue into False Claims Act exp=
osure
Upcoming Earnings
In the News
Executive View
The 2027 Star Ratings created a concentrated rather than industrywide improve=
ment.
Enrollment in four-star-or-better MA-PD contracts increased, even as the numb=
er of qualifying contracts and the enrollment-weighted average rating decline=
d.
Humana resolved its largest visible 2028 revenue uncertainty favorably.
Ninety-five percent of its MA members are now in four-star-or-better contract=
s, but management indicated that performance above its targeted level is part=
ly nonrecurring and will not automatically fund permanent benefits or expense=
s.
McKesson=E2=80=99s investment in Option Care converts home infusion from a cu=
stomer channel into a potential ownership position.
The structure preserves Option Care=E2=80=99s independence initially but give=
s McKesson a framework to acquire full control later.
Risk-adjustment enforcement moved deeper into deletion workflow.
A new False Claims Act settlement treats unresolved suspect diagnoses and agi=
ng =E2=80=9Cdelete=E2=80=9D queues as compliance exposure, even where coders =
originally identified the problem.
Overall environment: improved for Humana=E2=80=99s 2028 MA economics and alte=
rnate-site infusion capital; deteriorated for plans with weaker Stars perform=
ance or poorly governed risk-adjustment corrections; broadly unchanged in agg=
regate near-term.
1. Humana=E2=80=99s Stars recovery creates a two-speed 2028 quality market
Status:
RESOLVE
What happened
CMS=E2=80=99s October 8 release shows approximately 71% of current MA-PD enro=
llment in contracts rated four stars or higher for 2027, up from roughly 67% =
for 2026. That apparent improvement is unusually concentrated: qualifying con=
tracts fell from 229 to 188, or from about 44% to 37% of rated contracts, whi=
le the enrollment-weighted average slipped from 4.01 to 3.99 stars. The ratin=
gs affect 2028 quality-bonus payments. (
CMS, =E2=80=9C2027 Medicare Advantage and Part D Star Ratings,=E2=80=9D Octob=
er 8, 2026
) (
cms.gov
)
Humana drove much of the enrollment shift. It reported that 95% of its MA mem=
bers are in four-star-or-better plans and 42% are in 4.5-star plans, versus t=
he 20% it disclosed for the prior rating cycle using then-current membership.=
 Humana added 11 four-star-or-better contracts and said its normalized Stars =
revenue per member exceeded its target of at least 10% above the median of Ce=
ntene, CVS, Elevance, HCSC and UnitedHealth. (
Humana, =E2=80=9CHumana Announces Improved CMS Star Ratings,=E2=80=9D October=
 9, 2026
;
Humana Form 8-K, October 9, 2026
) (
policy.humana.com
)
Implication
The new information resolves a major component of Humana=E2=80=99s 2028 recov=
ery thesis: quality revenue is now likely to become a tailwind rather than an=
other margin-repair requirement. Humana nevertheless said the outperformance =
above its targeted =E2=80=9Ctop quartile=E2=80=9D level is partly one-time an=
d expects to direct that excess toward temporary investments and shareholder =
returns. The upside therefore should not be assumed to flow fully into recurr=
ing supplemental benefits or operating expense.
Industrywide, the divergence raises the value of contract-level=E2=80=94not p=
arent-level=E2=80=94Stars analytics. Plans below four stars must compete agai=
nst carriers with more rebate funding while simultaneously managing the benef=
it reductions and portfolio pruning identified last week. Demand should remai=
n strong for care-gap closure, member engagement, medication adherence and pr=
ovider-performance infrastructure, but spending capacity will increasingly di=
ffer by contract.
Watch next
Whether the ratings advantage translates into Humana retention and competitiv=
e switching during open enrollment, and whether lower-rated carriers consolid=
ate membership into stronger contracts ahead of 2028.
2. McKesson gains a path from specialty distribution into home-infusion owner=
ship
Status:
NEW
What happened
CD&R and McKesson agreed on October 5 to acquire Option Care Health for $32.0=
5 per share, representing approximately $5.8 billion of enterprise value. CD&=
R would own roughly 51%; McKesson would invest about $1.4 billion for approxi=
mately 49%. Option Care will remain separately managed, but the agreement est=
ablishes a framework under which McKesson could later acquire CD&R=E2=80=99s =
interest, subject to conditions and regulatory approvals. Closing is expected=
 in the first half of 2027. (
Option Care Health Form 8-K, filed October 6, 2026
;
CD&R, McKesson and Option Care Health, joint announcement, October 6, 2026
) (
sec.gov
)
Option Care describes itself as the largest independent U.S. provider of home=
 and alternate-site infusion services, with operations in all 50 states and m=
ore than 5,000 clinicians. The company withdrew its prior 2026 guidance follo=
wing the transaction announcement. (
sec.gov
)
Implication
The transaction creates a potentially important vertical control point betwee=
n specialty-drug supply and lower-cost administration. For payers, a scaled p=
latform connecting drug access, benefit verification, prior authorization, di=
spensing and home or ambulatory infusion could accelerate site-of-care progra=
ms that redirect eligible therapy from hospital outpatient departments.
For McKesson, the initial minority structure limits immediate integration, bu=
t the path to full ownership is strategically more significant than a passive=
 financial investment. It provides potential access to Option Care=E2=80=99s =
national clinical footprint and relationships with payers, health systems and=
 manufacturers. Hospital infusion businesses face a better-capitalized compet=
itor; workflow vendors should see demand around referral intake, inventory, s=
cheduling, claims and patient-responsibility coordination.
The principal uncertainty is whether regulators or commercial partners view t=
he combination as improving distribution efficiency or concentrating too much=
 influence over specialty-drug sourcing and administration.
Watch next
The proxy materials, healthcare-regulatory filings and any conditions placed =
on data sharing, manufacturer relationships or McKesson=E2=80=99s future acqu=
isition rights.
3. DOJ turns an unresolved diagnosis-deletion queue into False Claims Act exp=
osure
Status:
UPDATE
What happened
AIDS Healthcare Foundation agreed to pay $1.44 million to resolve allegations=
 concerning its Medicare Advantage special-needs plans. DOJ alleged that risk=
-adjustment coders maintained =E2=80=9CDelete Research=E2=80=9D spreadsheets =
listing diagnoses that potentially lacked medical-record support, but that AH=
F failed to investigate and delete many codes within the required 60-day peri=
od. Most corrections were not submitted until 2024 or 2025, after the organiz=
ation learned of the federal investigation. The settlement covers payment yea=
rs 2017 through 2023 and includes no determination of liability. (
Department of Justice, =E2=80=9CAIDS Healthcare Foundation Agrees to Pay $1.4=
4M to Settle False Claims Act Allegations,=E2=80=9D October 6, 2026
) (
justice.gov
)
Implication
The dollar amount is small, but the operating-control theory is consequential=
. Risk-adjustment liability does not end when coding staff identify a questio=
nable diagnosis; the identification can start an auditable remediation clock.=
 A spreadsheet or analytics queue that flags suspect codes without documented=
 investigation, escalation and deletion may become evidence that an organizat=
ion retained known overpayments.
Plans and vendors need bidirectional workflows that treat deletes with the sa=
me rigor as additions: evidence capture, ownership, aging, escalation, submis=
sion confirmation and reconciliation to CMS payment. Contracts with chart-rev=
iew, coding and in-home-assessment vendors should also specify who investigat=
es conflicts and who is responsible for timely correction.
Watch next
Whether subsequent DOJ or OIG actions apply the same reasoning to larger carr=
iers or downstream coding vendors, particularly where unsupported diagnoses w=
ere internally flagged but left unresolved.
What I'm Watching Next
October 15, 2026:
Medicare annual open enrollment begins, testing whether Humana=E2=80=99s Star=
s recovery and competitors=E2=80=99 localized plan exits change member flows.=
 (
cms.gov
)
November 4, 2026:
Option Care Health plans to report third-quarter results without a conference=
 call; prior guidance has been withdrawn. (
sec.gov
)
December 10, 2026:
Humana=E2=80=99s investor update is scheduled to mark progress against its 20=
28 framework, although detailed use of the excess Stars benefit may not be av=
ailable until its fourth-quarter 2027 call. (
Humana Investor Relations, event calendar
) (
sec.gov
)
Bottom Line
The week=E2=80=99s most important change is Humana=E2=80=99s 2027 Stars recov=
ery, which materially improves its 2028 quality-revenue position while reveal=
ing that aggregate industry improvement is concentrated in a shrinking group =
of contracts. Contract-level Stars exposure requires the closest attention. O=
pen enrollment beginning October 15 is the next event most likely to show whe=
ther quality differentiation translates into enrollment advantage rather than=
 merely future bonus revenue.
