<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-3" name=3D"story-3" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>3. DOJ turns an unresolved diagno=
sis-deletion queue into False Claims Act exposure</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Status:</strong> UPDATE</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">AIDS Healthcare Foundation agreed to p=
ay $1.44 million to resolve allegations concerning its Medicare Advantage spe=
cial-needs plans. DOJ alleged that risk-adjustment coders maintained =E2=80=
=9CDelete Research=E2=80=9D spreadsheets listing diagnoses that potentially l=
acked medical-record support, but that AHF failed to investigate and delete m=
any codes within the required 60-day period. Most corrections were not submit=
ted until 2024 or 2025, after the organization learned of the federal investi=
gation. The settlement covers payment years 2017 through 2023 and includes no=
 determination of liability. (<a href=3D"https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/aids-=
healthcare-foundation-agrees-pay-144m-settle-false-claims-act-allegations" st=
yle=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">Department of Justice, =E2=
=80=9CAIDS Healthcare Foundation Agrees to Pay $1.44M to Settle False Claims =
Act Allegations,=E2=80=9D October 6, 2026</a>) (<a href=3D"https://www.justic=
e.gov/opa/pr/aids-healthcare-foundation-agrees-pay-144m-settle-false-claims-a=
ct-allegations?utm_source=3Dopenai" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:un=
derline;">justice.gov</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The dollar amount is small, but the op=
erating-control theory is consequential. Risk-adjustment liability does not e=
nd when coding staff identify a questionable diagnosis; the identification ca=
n start an auditable remediation clock. A spreadsheet or analytics queue that=
 flags suspect codes without documented investigation, escalation and deletio=
n may become evidence that an organization retained known overpayments.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Plans and vendors need bidirectional w=
orkflows that treat deletes with the same rigor as additions: evidence captur=
e, ownership, aging, escalation, submission confirmation and reconciliation t=
o CMS payment. Contracts with chart-review, coding and in-home-assessment ven=
dors should also specify who investigates conflicts and who is responsible fo=
r timely correction.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Whether subsequent DOJ or OIG actions =
apply the same reasoning to larger carriers or downstream coding vendors, par=
ticularly where unsupported diagnoses were internally flagged but left unreso=
lved.</p>
